{
  "dom_id": "step-q347261",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$",
